  "transcript": [
    "> The 'references/python-anti-patterns.md' file is missing the anti-pattern of decoding bytes/strings documented in 'references/nodejs-anti-patterns.md', but it does make mention of it in the 'references/python-decode-docs.md' file.  Update this Python-specific anti-patterns document to account for that.",
    "I'll read the relevant files to understand the anti-pattern and then update the Python anti-patterns document.\n[Tool uses: fs_read]",
    "Now I'll update the Python anti-patterns document to include the binary data decoding anti-pattern:\n[Tool uses: fs_write]",
    "> t",
    "Done! I've added the binary data handling anti-pattern to the Python anti-patterns document. The new section shows:\n\n- **❌ INCORRECT**: Not decoding bytes from search results (returns `b'doc:1'` instead of `'doc:1'`)\n- **✅ CORRECT**: Properly decoding bytes to strings while skipping binary fields like embeddings\n\nThis matches the pattern documented in Node.js anti-patterns and aligns with the decode logic shown in `python-decode-docs.md`.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Make it more brief and tell the reader to refer to the 'references/python-decode-docs.md' for details.",
    "\n[Tool uses: fs_write]",
    "Done! I've made the section more concise and added a reference to `references/python-decode-docs.md` for the complete implementation details.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Two problems with the Python implementation that this skill needs to point out as a potential pitfall.  First, ensure you understand the method prototype for these two GLIDE API methods listed, then update the 'references/python.md' file's \"Common Pitfalls\" section accordingly:> \n> \nGLIDE's ping() returns b'PONG' (bytes), not a bool.> \nGLIDE's scan() requires a string cursor; e.g. cursor = 0 (int) will be rejected. Change to a string wrapped integer (e.g. \"0\")",
    "I'll first check the current \"Common Pitfalls\" section in the Python reference, then add these two pitfalls.\n[Tool uses: fs_read]",
    "\n[Tool uses: fs_read]",
    "Now I'll add the two new pitfalls to the Common Pitfalls section:\n[Tool uses: fs_write]",
    "Done! I've added two new pitfalls to the \"Common Pitfalls\" section:\n\n**Pitfall #9**: `ping()` returns `b'PONG'` (bytes), not a boolean\n**Pitfall #10**: `scan()` requires a string cursor (e.g., `\"0\"`), not an integer\n\nThese are now documented alongside the existing 8 pitfalls in the Python reference guide.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Reformat this \"Common Pitfalls\" section into a table with three columns: \"Description\", \"Problem\" and \"Solution\"",
    "\n[Tool uses: fs_write]",
    "Done! I've reformatted the \"Common Pitfalls\" section into a table with three columns: Description, Problem, and Solution. All 10 pitfalls are now presented in a more scannable format.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Are the two bool/ping() and integer/scan() pitfalls documented in the 'references/python-anti-patterns.md' file? or anywhere else in 'references/python.md'?",
    "\n[Tool uses: grep]",
    "No, the `ping()` and `scan()` pitfalls are only documented in the Common Pitfalls table we just added to `references/python.md`. They are not documented in `references/python-anti-patterns.md` or anywhere else in the Python reference files.\n[Tool uses: none]",
