    "## C# Testing Complete - Excellent Results! ✅\n\n**All 6 C# test cases passed** with idiomatic C# patterns on the first try:\n\n1. ✅ **Client creation** - StandaloneClientConfigurationBuilder, TimeSpan timeout, async/await\n2. ✅ **Batch operations** - MGet, Dictionary<string, string>, null handling\n3. ✅ **Code review** - Identified resource leak, wrong method names (StringSetAsync/StringGetAsync), missing error handling\n4. ✅ **Cluster operations** - Hash tags, ClusterBatch(raiseOnError: true), GlideClusterClient\n5. ✅ **Binary data** - byte[] handling, await using, null handling\n6. ✅ **Scan keys** - Cursor iteration, do-while pattern, proper async\n\n**Why C# succeeded:**\n- **await using** - Most concise disposal pattern\n- **Type safety** - Can't call non-existent methods\n- **Task<T>** - Unified async pattern\n- **TimeSpan** - Type-safe timeout configuration\n\n## Final Validation Summary\n\n**✅ Type-Safe Languages (Production-Ready):**\n- **Java**: try-with-resources, CompletableFuture, explicit timeouts\n- **Go**: defer, explicit error handling, context propagation\n- **C#**: await using, Task<T>, TimeSpan timeouts\n\n**⚠️ Dynamic Language (Has Issues):**\n- **Python**: Persistent `client.ft_*()` API hallucination despite 4 iterations of corrections\n\n**Conclusion**: The Valkey GLIDE skill is **production-ready for type-safe languages** (Java, Go, C#). All three produce correct, idiomatic code immediately. Python has a documented limitation where the AI hallucinates `client.ft_search()` and `client.ft_create()` methods that don't exist in the API.\n\n**Recommendation**: Mark the skill as validated for Java, Go, and C#. Document the Python limitation and consider it a known issue with dynamic language skills.\n[Tool uses: none]",
