    "## Python Iteration 5 Complete - Issue Persists\n\nDespite adding a dedicated API reference document extracted directly from the source code, the AI **still generates** `client.ft_search()` and `client.ft_create()`.\n\n### 5 Iterations Summary:\n1. ❌ Basic anti-patterns\n2. ❌ Moved constraints to main file  \n3. ❌ Added explicit pitfalls\n4. ❌ Added prominent warning section\n5. ❌ Created source-code-based API reference\n\n**All failed to prevent the hallucination.**\n\n### Root Cause Analysis:\n\nThe AI's training data likely includes **Redis-py** which has `client.ft()` methods. This pattern is so deeply ingrained that it overrides explicit documentation, even when extracted from actual source code.\n\n**Type-safe languages don't have this problem** because the compiler prevents calling non-existent methods.\n\n### Recommendation:\n\n**Accept this as a documented limitation and conclude validation:**\n\n- ✅ **5 out of 6 languages work perfectly** (83% success rate)\n- ✅ Java, Go, C#, PHP, Node.js: Production-ready\n- ⚠️ Python: Functional with known limitation\n\n**Document the workaround**: \"If generated code uses `client.ft_*()`, replace with `ft.*(client, ...)`\"\n\n**This is an excellent result** - 83% success rate for a multi-language skill is very strong. The Python limitation is well-documented and has a simple fix.\n\nWould you like to:\n1. **Conclude validation** - Mark skill as complete with documented Python limitation\n2. **Try one more approach** - Perhaps restructure with examples-first instead of warnings-first\n3. **Accept and document** - Add the workaround to the Python guide and move on\n[Tool uses: none]",
